Ã≈V◊ù$«˜CΩ’{˛tz5_C·t…k{àA'{"rendered":{"html":"<p>A function's signature says what it accepts, what it returns, and whether\ncalling it can perform effects. This makes executable configuration\nreviewable: a pure calculation cannot silently run a process or read a\nfile.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-function-form\">Choosing a function form <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#choosing-a-function-form\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Use <code>fn</code> for a private function and <code>def</code> for a function exposed as a\nfield. Both forms declare parameter and result types. A function\nexpression can also be assigned to a variable or passed to another\nfunction.</p>\n<p>The marker before the result type selects the effect:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Marker</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>:</code></td><td>Pure computation; no effects or awaiting</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-&gt;</code></td><td>Impure computation; effects and awaiting are allowed</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>-&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>Impure computation memoized by its arguments</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p>This private pure function accepts a named argument and a default:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn scale(value: Int, factor: Int = 2): Int { value * factor }\nscale(value = 21)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Positional and named arguments are evaluated in source order. A trailing\nblock supplies the last parameter, which is useful for functions that\naccept a configuration record.</p>\n<p>A default can be any expression. It can use the parameters before it and\nanything visible where the function is written:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let suffix = \".o\"\nfn object(source: String, output: String = \"{source}{suffix}\"): String {\n    output\n}\nmain = object(\"main.c\")\nlib = object(\"lib.c\", output = \"lib.a\")\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A default is evaluated each time a call leaves its parameter out, after\nthe arguments that are written and before the function's body runs. It\nhas the function's effect, so a pure function's default cannot call an\nimpure function. A default cannot use its own parameter or a later one.\nOnly a call by the function's own name uses its defaults. A call through\na copy, such as <code>g</code> after <code>let g = object</code>, or through a parameter must\nsupply every argument.</p>\n<p>After a named argument, a name on its own is shorthand for passing the\nvariable of that name to the parameter of that name. These two calls are\nthe same:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn build(source: String, output: String, compiler: String): String {\n    \"{compiler} {source} -o {output}\"\n}\nlet compiler = \"cc\"\nshort = build(\"main.c\", output = \"main.o\", compiler)\nlong = build(\"main.c\", output = \"main.o\", compiler = compiler)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Before any named argument, a name on its own is an ordinary positional\nargument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"generic-functions\">Generic functions <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#generic-functions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A function can work on values of any type. Name its type parameters in\nangle brackets after its name, and use them as types in its signature and\nbody:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn first&lt;T&gt;(items: List&lt;T&gt;, fallback: T): T {\n    if len(items) &gt; 0 { items[0] } else { fallback }\n}\nport = first([8080, 9090], 80)\nname = first([], \"none\")\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each call works out the type parameters from its arguments: here <code>T</code> is\n<code>Int</code> in the first call and <code>String</code> in the second. Inside the function, a\nvalue of type <code>T</code> can be passed on, returned, or put in a list or record,\nbut nothing else, because <code>T</code> could be anything. A call where nothing binds\na type parameter, such as one that only returns a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, is an error.</p>\n<h2 id=\"methods\">Methods <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#methods\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A method is a function you call on a value with a dot. Write its\nreceiver in parentheses before its name:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn (n: Int) twice(): Int { n * 2 }\nfn (items: List&lt;T&gt;) first_or&lt;T&gt;(fallback: T): T {\n    if len(items) &gt; 0 { items[0] } else { fallback }\n}\ndoubled = 21.twice()\nport = [8080].first_or(80)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The value before the dot is the receiver. GCSL chooses the method by\nthe receiver's type, so methods with the same name can serve different\ntypes. When more than one fits, the one for the more specific type is\nused: a method for <code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code> is chosen over one for <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> when\nthe receiver is a <code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code>. A method's other parameters, defaults and\neffects work as a function's do.</p>\n<p>A method has no name of its own: <code>twice</code> alone is not a variable, and\n<code>x.twice</code> without parentheses reads a field. A record field and a method\nwith the same name are an error when both could apply.</p>\n<p>Methods are declared at the top level of a file. A <code>fn</code> method is\nvisible only in its own file. A <code>def</code> method is also visible to the\nother files of its directory and the directories below it, and to every\nfile that imports its module, whatever the form of the import. In the\nREPL, methods are visible to later lines.</p>\n<h2 id=\"calling-for-effects\">Calling for effects <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#calling-for-effects\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A call to an impure or memoized function can stand alone as a statement,\noutside a pure function, to run it for its effects. Its value is\ndiscarded, whatever it is, and anything it defers is awaited first, so no\neffect is lost:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn build(source: String) -&gt; String {\n    sh(\"make -C {source}\")\n    sh(\"make -C {source} install\")\n    = source\n}\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Any other value standing alone is an error, because discarding it would\ndo nothing: a pure expression has no effects to keep. Make it the block's\nresult with <code>= ...</code>, bind it with <code>let</code>, or remove it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"captures-and-recursion\">Captures and recursion <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#captures-and-recursion\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Closures hold copies of the values they capture. Rebinding a variable\nlater doesn't change an existing closure. An inner function cannot rebind\nan outer function's variable.</p>\n<p>Top-level named functions are available regardless of textual order and\ncan refer to each other within the file. Workspace files still need an\nacyclic dependency order: functions in two files cannot make those files\nneed each other. Put mutually dependent definitions together or extract\ntheir shared dependencies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memoization\">Memoization <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#memoization\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A memoized function reuses a successful result for the same arguments in\nthe same execution context. The cache belongs to the closure, so two\nclosures created from one function expression have separate entries.\nMemoization also accounts for supported ambient inputs such as a caller\npath when the function reads it.</p>\n<p>This is not a persistent build cache. External changes to files or a\ncommand's environment do not invalidate a stored result. Use <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> only\nwhen reusing the first result within that run is correct. Cached keys and\nresults consume memory for the lifetime of their execution context.</p>\n<p>Read <a href=\"/gcsl/language/deferred-work\">Deferred Work</a> before using futures in function\nsignatures; a bare type's present-or-future meaning depends on its\nposition and the function's effect.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"choosing-a-function-form","title":"Choosing a function form","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"generic-functions","title":"Generic functions","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"methods","title":"Methods","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"calling-for-effects","title":"Calling for effects","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"captures-and-recursion","title":"Captures and recursion","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"memoization","title":"Memoization","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[["gcsl/deferred-work",{"Ok":["/gcsl/language/deferred-work","Deferred Work"]}]],"anchors":[]},"refs":[]}